{
  "image_name": "e08e32123fa7f026182d3138ad45bd74.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes ways to modify a porous cellular material, likely wood, through physical, chemical, and combined treatments. Across the top, physical modification is shown as changing the bulk structure without altering chemistry: the material can be densified, patterned into a lattice-like architecture, or given a surface coating.\n\nThe middle section focuses on chemical modification at the cell and cell-wall level. Additive approaches introduce new matter into the structure, including filling the lumen, coating internal surfaces, grafting functional groups onto the framework, or filling the cell wall itself. Subtractive approaches remove or transform existing components, as in delignification, which either preserves the original cellular network or leaves a more distorted, wavy framework; other examples include removing functional groups and carbonization, which converts the structure into a darker carbon-like scaffold.\n\nThe bottom row highlights combined strategies that pair these methods to create more tailored materials. Examples include delignification followed by lumen filling, grafting, or densification, as well as carbonization combined with coating. Overall, the figure conveys that the same hierarchical cellular scaffold can be tuned by adding, removing, reshaping, or combining treatments to control structure and function.",
